
{"id":"7ba81aaf-b8f9-4f2a-bbcd-bf043f5cae7b","object":"chat.completion","created":1784812150,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","reasoning_content":"We need to produce JSON with key \"implementation\" and value a Python source string for solve(case). The task: implement chunk_reduction_schedule. From description: apply deterministic chunk-removal and granularity-refinement passes. Start chunk removal at granularity 2. Try chunks from left to right, accept removal only if required failure items remain. Restart after acceptance with slightly coarser granularity (increase? \"slightly coarser\" means larger granularity? It says \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\" So when a removal succeeds, we restart with slightly coarser? That seems contradictory: \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" Then later \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\". So algorithm: start granularity = 2. While granularity <= len(items) (or until singleton? Actually stop at singleton granularity, i.e., granularity 1? But start at 2, double when no removal, restart with slightly coarser after removal. Need to clarify.\n\nWe have items list and required_failure_items list. Goal: reduce items (remove chunks) while preserving that required failure items remain. The procedure: chunk removal at granularity g means we split items into chunks of size g? Or overlapping? \"Try chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" Likely we try removing each contiguous chunk of length g from the current items list, left to right, and if after removal the required failure items are still all present (i.e., each required item appears at least once), then accept that removal and restart with slightly coarser granularity. \"Slightly coarser\" likely means increase granularity by 1? Or by some factor? The phrase \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" suggests that after a removal we restart with a slightly coarser (maybe g+1) but also we have doubling when no removal succeeds. So the algorithm might be: start g=2. While g <= len(items) and g >= 2? Actually stop at singleton granularity (g=1). But if we double when no removal, we might skip some values. Let's read carefully:\n\n\"Start chunk removal at granularity two.\nTry chunks from left to right and accept removal only if required failure items remain.\nRestart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\nDouble granularity when no removal succeeds and stop at singleton granularity.\"\n\nInterpretation: We have current granularity g. We attempt to remove chunks of size g from the current items list. If any chunk removal succeeds (i.e., after removal, all required failure items are still present), we accept the first such removal (left to right), then restart with a slightly coarser granularity. \"Slightly coarser\" could be g+1? Or maybe g*2? But then \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" suggests that when no removal succeeds at current g, we double g (g = g*2). And stop when g becomes 1 (singleton). But starting at 2, doubling would go 2,4,8,... That might skip 3,5, etc. But \"slightly coarser\" after success suggests a smaller increment. Possibly the intended algorithm is:\n\n- Start g = 2.\n- While granularity <= len(items) and granularity >= 2:\n   - For each possible contiguous chunk of size g in current items (left to right):\n        - If removing that chunk yields a list that still contains all required failure items:\n            - Accept removal (update items), then set granularity = g + 1? (slightly coarser)\n            - Break out of chunk loop and continue while loop with new items and new granularity.\n   - If no chunk removal accepted at this granularity, then double granularity: g = g * 2.\n- Continue until g > len(items) or g becomes 1? \"stop at singleton granularity\" meaning stop when g becomes 1? But we start at 2, and if we double, we might exceed len(items) and then stop? However, the instruction says \"stop at singleton granularity\", i.e., we stop when granularity == 1? But we never go to 1 unless we double from 2? 2*2=4, not 1. So maybe \"Double granularity\" actually means increase by factor? Or maybe \"slightly coarser\" means increase by 1, and \"double\" means multiply by 2 only when no removal succeeds and we need to jump to larger chunks? The phrase \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean we stop the process when we have tried granularity = 1 (i.e., individual items). But if we start at 2 and only double when no removal, we would never try 3,5,6,7,... So that seems unlikely.\n\nAlternative interpretation: Start with granularity g=2. Perform attempts: try removing each chunk of size g. If any removal succeeds, restart with granularity g+1 (slightly coarser). If no removal succeeds, double granularity (g*2). Continue until g becomes 1 (singleton). But how would g become 1? If we double from 2, we get 4, then 8, etc. Never 1. So maybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means stop when the granularity is larger than the number of items? Or when the items cannot be divided into chunks of size g? But then \"singleton granularity\" might refer to the case where each item is its own chunk (granularity=1). But we never try 1.\n\nMaybe the algorithm is more like: We have a current granularity. We try to remove chunks of that size. If we succeed, we increase granularity by 1 (coarser). If we fail, we increase granularity by 1 as well? No.\n\nPerhaps \"Double granularity\" means multiply by 2, and \"slightly coarser\" means increase by 1? That would mean two different increments. But the instruction says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That implies after a success, we increase granularity by a small amount (say 1). Then \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" implies when all chunks fail, we increase by a larger factor (double). And \"stop at singleton granularity\" means we stop when granularity becomes 1? That would only happen if we start at 2 and double? No.\n\nMaybe \"singleton granularity\" means granularity of 1, i.e., individual items. If we start at 2 and no removal succeeds, we double to 4, then if no removal at 4, double to 8, etc. Eventually when len(items) < 2, we stop? But then we never try 1. So maybe we stop when granularity > len(items) (i.e., we cannot form a chunk of that size). Then the final items are the reduced set.\n\nBut the description says \"stop at singleton granularity\" likely means the algorithm stops when granularity reaches 1 (one item per chunk). However, with doubling, we may skip over 1. So perhaps \"slightly coarser\" means we increment by 1, and \"double\" means we multiply by 2 only when no removal and we have not yet reached 1? But starting at 2, if we succeed, g becomes 3, then 4, etc. If we fail, we double: from 2 to 4, from 4 to 8, from 8 to 16, etc. That would eventually exceed length and stop. But then we never try 1. The requirement to \"stop at singleton granularity\" might be misinterpreted: maybe it means that when we have reduced to a single item (the minimum), we stop. That is, the process ends when the granularity becomes 1 (i.e., each chunk is a single item) and we have tried all possible individual removals? But the algorithm as described doesn't attempt individual removals.\n\nLet's look at the fixtures: They give items list of 10 elements, and required_failure_items list of 2. So we need to remove as many chunks as possible while preserving those 2. Typically such reduction algorithms try larger chunks first. Starting at granularity 2 means trying to remove pairs. If that fails, double to 4 (try quadruples). If that fails, double to 8, etc. When a removal succeeds, restart with a slightly coarser granularity (maybe the same size? Or maybe increase by 1? \"Slightly coarser\" suggests a small increase, so likely +1. But the algorithm must be deterministic and clear.\n\nI think the intended algorithm is a variation of delta debugging (like ddmin). In delta debugging, you start with a granularity of 2, try to remove each half, then if successful you decrease granularity (i.e., increase number of subsets?). Actually in typical delta debugging, you have a set of tests. You partition into n subsets. Here they say \"chunk removal\" meaning you remove contiguous chunks. And granularity is the size of chunks. Starting at 2 means you try removing chunks of 2 items. If any removal works, you restart with a slightly coarser granularity (i.e., larger chunks) because you want to remove bigger chunks. That matches \"slightly coarser\" = increase chunk size. If no removal works, you double the granularity (jump to larger chunks) to try bigger chunks. And you stop when granularity becomes 1 (i.e., you are trying to remove single items). But how do you get to 1? If you start at 2 and always increase, you never get to 1. Unless \"singleton granularity\" means the case where you have reached the point where only one item remains? That doesn't fit.\n\nMaybe \"stop at singleton granularity\" means you stop the process when the granularity (chunk size) is 1, but the algorithm only decreases granularity? Wait: \"Start chunk removal at granularity two. Try chunks... Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That means after success, granularity increases (coarser). \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" also increases. So granularity only increases. You start at 2, then only increase. So you will eventually reach a granularity larger than the number of items, then you stop. That would be the termination condition. But then \"stop at singleton granularity\" doesn't match. Perhaps \"singleton granularity\" means that when the granularity is 1, you stop because you've tried individual removals. But you never try 1. So maybe \"double granularity\" is actually a decrease? \"Double\" might be ambiguous: \"Double granularity\" could mean make it finer (i.e., half the size). In typical delta debugging, when a removal fails, you decrease the granularity (i.e., make chunks smaller). For example, start with granularity 2 (half), if removal succeeds, you keep granularity? Actually typical ddmin: you partition into n subsets. If any subset fails (i.e., removal cause failure), you reduce the set to that subset and start again with n=2. If no subset fails, you increase n (finer granularity). That's the opposite. Here they say \"accept removal only if required failure items remain\" which is like \"interestingness preserved\". So removal is accepted if the result still contains required items (i.e., the failure is preserved). So if removal succeeds (failure preserved), you want to keep that reduction and then try larger chunks (coarser) to remove more. That matches \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" If no removal succeeds (i.e., removing any chunk of that size removes a required failure item), then you need to try smaller chunks (finer granularity) to isolate which specific items are critical. That would be \"Double granularity\" meaning multiply by 2? That would make chunks larger, not smaller. So maybe \"Double granularity\" means divide by 2 (i.e., make it finer). But the phrase \"double\" usually means increase. However, in some contexts, \"double the granularity\" could mean make it twice as fine (i.e., half the size). For example, \"increase granularity\" often means finer resolution. So it's ambiguous.\n\nGiven the task is to implement `chunk_reduction_schedule`, likely it's based on the classic \"DDMin\" algorithm but with contiguous chunks. The typical DDMin algorithm: start with n=2, try each of the n subsets (ordered). If any subset causes the failure, recurse on that subset with n=2. If no subset causes failure, increase n to min(2n, len(set)). Continue until n >= len(set). This is for minimizing a set. Here they have \"chunk removal\" meaning removing chunks from the current items, not subsets. But comparable: they want to remove as many items as possible while preserving the failure (required items). So they try to remove chunks of increasing size? Actually they start with size 2, try each chunk. If removing a chunk preserves required items, they accept it and then try again with same size? Or coarser? The text says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" So after a removal, they increase chunk size (coarser) to try to remove larger chunks. That makes sense: after removing a small chunk, you try to remove larger chunks. If no removal works at a given size, they double the granularity (make it coarser) to try even larger chunks? That seems backward: if no removal works, larger chunks are even less likely to work because they would remove more items. Actually if a chunk of size 2 fails (i.e., removing it removes a required item), then a chunk of size 4 will also fail because it contains that required item. So you want to go finer, not coarser. So the correct behavior is: when no removal succeeds, you try smaller chunks (finer granularity). So \"Double granularity\" should mean make it finer (divide by 2). And \"slightly coarser\" after success means increase chunk size. So likely: start g=2. While g <= len(items) and g>=1: try removing each chunk of size g. If any succeeds, accept first, set g = g*2? Actually \"slightly coarser\" suggests increase by 1 or multiply by 2? \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" would mean make it finer (divide by 2). So the algorithm could be:\n\n- g = 2\n- while g >= 1 and g <= len(items):\n    - attempt to remove each chunk of size g from current items (left to right)\n    - if a removal succeeds:\n        - update items (remove that chunk)\n        - set g = g + 1 (slightly coarser) -- or maybe g = g*2? But \"slightly coarser\" suggests smaller increment.\n        - break and restart loop\n    - else:\n        - set g = g // 2 (or double in the sense of making finer? Actually dividing by 2 makes g finer if g>1. But if g=2, g//2 =1. Then we would try individual items. That matches \"stop at singleton granularity\" – when g becomes 1, that's singleton, and we try individual items. If no removal at g=1, then the algorithm ends.\n- continue until g < 1 or g > len(items).\n\nHowever, the text says \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\". In common language, \"double granularity\" means increase the size of the granules (make them larger). But here we want to make them smaller to try finer removal. So maybe \"double\" is used incorrectly? Alternatively, \"granularity\" might refer to the number of chunks? For instance, granularity could be the number of chunks you split into. Starting with 2 chunks, then 4, etc. That would be typical: start with 2 chunks, try each chunk. If a chunk removal works, restart with 2 chunks on the new set. If not, increase number of chunks to 4, etc. That is the typical ddmin algorithm. But the term \"chunk removal\" and \"granularity two\" points to chunk size, not number of pieces. \"Start chunk removal at granularity two.\" That suggests chunks of size 2. \"Double granularity\" would then mean chunks of size 4. That's coarser. So after a success, they \"restart with slightly coarser granularity\" which could mean increase by 1 or double. But then if success leads to larger chunks, that's fine: you want to remove larger chunks if possible. But when no removal works, you double the chunk size, making even larger chunks, which would almost certainly fail. That doesn't help. So there is a contradiction.\n\nLet's look at the examples: The fixture cases have 10 items, required failures are two items. In each case, the required items are different positions. The algorithm should produce a reduced list. Since required items are only two, the optimal reduction would remove all items except those two, but only if removing any chunk that contains neither required item is allowed. Starting at granularity 2 (size 2 chunks), you could remove many pairs. If you succeed and then increase granularity to 3, you might remove triplets. Eventually you might get down to just the two required items. That seems plausible. If no removal at size 2, doubling to size 4 might remove a larger chunk that includes a required item, so it fails. So you would keep doubling until the chunk size exceeds the list length and you stop. But then you would not remove anything. That would not produce a reduction. So clearly the algorithm must make finer granularity when no removal succeeds.\n\nGiven the confusion, I need to infer the intended algorithm from typical reduction tasks. The task ID SE-CR-02 likely corresponds to a known problem. The phrase \"chunk reduction schedule\" and the description of \"granularity progression\" and \"singleton granularity\" suggests a variant of delta debugging where you start with large chunks and make them smaller when needed. But the text says \"Start chunk removal at granularity two.\" That is small. \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" means make chunks larger. \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" means also make chunks larger. So both branches increase chunk size. That would monotonically increase chunk size, so you would never try smaller chunks. That seems like a greedy algorithm: try to remove small chunks, if successful, try larger; if not, try even larger. That could eventually remove many items, but you might miss some opportunities because you never try smaller chunks after a failure. But you start small, so if a chunk of size 2 fails because it contains a required item, you move to size 4, which contains that item too, so it also fails, etc. So if the first chunk (size 2) that contains a required item fails, then all larger chunks that contain that item also fail, so no removal ever happens. That would leave the list unchanged. But in the fixture, the required items are at positions 0 and 9 in case 0. Removing a chunk of size 2 that includes t0 (positions 0-1) would remove t0, causing failure. So that chunk will be rejected. Then you try the next chunk of size 2 (positions 1-2) which does not include t0 or t9, so it succeeds. So removal is accepted. Then you restart with slightly coarser granularity (size 3). Then you try chunks of size 3. That might succeed. So the algorithm works.\n\nNow, what about \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\"? That would be used when you have tried all chunks of current size and none succeeded. For example, if after some reductions, you have a list where no chunk of size g can be removed without losing a required item, then you double g (make chunks larger) to try to remove larger chunks that might encompass non-required items while still preserving required items? But if no chunk of size g works, then a larger chunk includes that same set plus more, so it will also contain the required items that prevented removal. So it's even less likely to work. Unless the required items are sparse and larger chunks can skip them? For instance, if the required items are at positions 2 and 7 in a list of 10, and current list is [a,b,req1,c,d,e,req2,f,g]. A chunk of size 2 starting at index 0 (a,b) works fine. After removing that, now list is [req1,c,d,e,req2,f,g]. Then try chunks of size 2: [req1,c] fails because req1; [c,d] works? Yes, remove that, list becomes [req1,e,req2,f,g]. Continue. Eventually you get to a state where the list is just [req1,req2]. Now granularity is some value. If you try chunk of size 2 on two items, the only chunk is the entire list, which removes both required items, so it fails. So at that state, no removal of size 2 works. Then you double granularity to 4, but the list length is 2, so you can't have chunk size 4. So the algorithm would stop? Actually the condition might be to stop when granularity exceeds len(items). So that's fine.\n\nThus, the algorithm as described (increase granularity on both success and failure, but success increase by a little, failure increase by double) could work, and when no removal at current g, you try larger g (double), but that will likely also fail, so you keep doubling until g > len, then stop. So you end up with the reduced list from the last successful removal. That seems plausible.\n\nNow, we must also implement \"explicit one-minimal check\". This likely means after the reduction, we must verify that the resulting list is 1-minimal: i.e., no single item can be removed while preserving required items. That's the \"one-minimal\" property. The output should include a boolean field like \"is_one_minimal\" or \"one_minimal_check\". The description says \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" Also \"aggregate values from the returned primitive decisions rather than from a second approximation.\"\n\nThus, the solve function should return a dictionary with keys: \"reduced_items\", \"attempt_count\", \"one_minimal\". Or maybe \"reduced items\", \"attempt count\", \"explicit one-minimal check\" as per the text. Let's parse: \"Return the reduced items, attempt count, and an explicit one-minimal check.\" So likely keys: \"reduced_items\", \"attempts\", \"one_minimal\" (boolean). Additionally, maybe \"granularity_progression\" but that might be internal.\n\nThe output must be JSON-serializable. So we will return a dict.\n\nWe must not mutate input case. The case has payload with keys \"items\" and \"required_failure_items\". Also \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Extract items list and required set from case payload.\n- Initialize current_items = list(items)\n- Initialize attempts = 0\n- Initialize granularity = 2\n- While granularity <= len(current_items) and granularity >= 1? Actually we stop at singleton granularity, maybe when granularity == 1? But we start at 2, and we may never get to 1. The condition might be while granularity >= 1 (but we never decrease). However, if we double when no removal succeeds, we could get to 1 if we start at 2 and double? 2*2=4, no 1. So the condition \"stop at singleton granularity\" probably means we stop when granularity becomes 1, which would happen if we divide by 2 when no removal succeeds. So I'm leaning towards the interpretation where \"double granularity\" actually means make finer (divide by 2) when no removal succeeds. That makes more sense: if you can't remove any chunk of size g, you try smaller chunks (finer). And after a successful removal, you try slightly coarser (increase by 1 or double? Slightly coarser suggests increase by 1). And you stop when granularity becomes 1 (singleton). That is a classic delta debugging variant: start with granularity 2 (chunk size 2), try each chunk. If a removal succeeds, accept it and reset granularity to 2? No, they say \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" That suggests after success, you increase chunk size (coarser) because you want to remove even larger chunks. But if you reset to coarser, you might miss removing smaller chunks first. Actually typical algorithm: you try large chunks first, then if they fail, you go smaller. Here they start small, then go larger after success. That is also valid.\n\nGiven the ambiguity, I'll choose an algorithm that matches the description as literally as possible: start g=2. While g <= len(items) and g >= 2 (since we stop at singleton, we need to eventually get to 1). We'll allow g to be 1 as final step. Use:\n- If a removal succeeds: set g = g + 1 (slightly coarser) and restart loop (continue while).\n- If no removal succeeds: set g = g * 2 (double) and continue.\n- Stop when g > len(items) (since can't form chunk) or when g == 1? Actually if we double, we might exceed len quickly. We'll stop when no removal and g > len(items). But then we never try g=1. So we need to handle the case when g becomes 1 due to some operation. The instruction says \"stop at singleton granularity\", implying that we should continue until we have tried singletons (g=1). So we need a way to reach g=1. Perhaps \"double granularity\" means divide by 2 (i.e., finer) when no removal succeeds. Then starting at 2, if no removal at 2, we divide by 2 to get 1, then try singletons. That would hit singleton. After a success, we increase granularity (slightly coarser) e.g., add 1, resulting in 2 again? That would loop. But the algorithm should terminate.\n\nLet's examine the phrase \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\". The word \"double\" typically means multiply by 2. But maybe in this context, \"granularity\" refers to the chunk size, and \"double\" means make it twice as coarse, i.e., larger size. That doesn't lead to 1. Alternatively, \"granularity\" might refer to the number of chunks? For example, granularity 2 means split into 2 chunks. Then \"double granularity\" means split into 4 chunks (finer). That would lead to smaller chunks. That fits: start with 2 chunks (large chunks), if removal of a chunk works, you keep the same number of chunks? Actually typical ddmin: start with n=2 subsets, try each. If a subset fails (i.e., removal causes failure), you recurse on that subset with n=2. If no subset fails, you increase n to min(2n, len(set)). That is \"double granularity\" (increase number of subsets). That matches the classic algorithm. And \"slightly coarser granularity\" after success might be to set n=2 again? Not exactly.\n\nGiven the typical ddmin, the algorithm is:\n- set n = 2\n- while n <= len(current_set):\n    - partition current_set into n contiguous subsets (chunks) of roughly equal size.\n    - for each subset in order:\n        - test if removing that subset preserves failure.\n        - if yes, set current_set = current_set without that subset, set n = max(n-1, 2) (or set n=2 to restart), break.\n    - if no success, set n = min(2*n, len(current_set))\n- return current_set.\n\nBut the description says \"start chunk removal at granularity two.\" That could mean number of chunks = 2. \"Try chunks from left to right\" – that matches partitioning into 2 chunks (left and right). \"Accept removal only if required failure items remain\" – after removing a chunk, check. \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity\" – after a success, maybe increase number of chunks slightly (e.g., n+1) to get finer granularity? Actually more chunks means smaller chunks (finer). \"Slightly coarser\" might be a mistake: if you have more chunks, each chunk is smaller, so granularity is finer. So \"slightly coarser\" would mean fewer chunks (larger chunks). So after success, you want to try even larger chunks? That would be to decrease n. But they say \"slightly coarser\", so maybe n-1. \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" – if no removal works with current number of chunks, you want to try more chunks (finer), so double n. That matches typical ddmin: if no subset fails, increase n. And after a successful reduction, you reset n to 2 (or reduce it) to try larger subsets again. The phrase \"slightly coarser\" could mean set n to n-1 (slightly fewer chunks, i.e., larger chunks). That is plausible.\n\nThus, I think the intended algorithm is based on delta debugging with contiguous subsets, where \"granularity\" is the number of subsets (chunks). Starting with 2 subsets, try each. If a subset removal works, keep that reduction and then try with a slightly coarser granularity (i.e., fewer subsets, say n-1, but not below 2). If no removal works, double the number of subsets (finer granularity) up to the size of the current set. Stop when n exceeds the size (i.e., you've tried singletons). That yields the final reduced set.\n\nThis interpretation aligns with the classic ddmin algorithm. The description says \"start chunk removal at granularity two\" meaning 2 chunks. \"Try chunks from left to right\" – the chunks are the two halves (left and right). \"Accept removal only if required failure items remain.\" Then \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\" – after success, you increase chunk size (i.e., reduce number of chunks) to try to remove larger chunks. \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" – after failure, you increase number of chunks (smaller chunks). \"stop at singleton granularity\" – when n equals the size of the set (each chunk is a single element), you stop.\n\nYes! This makes perfect sense. So we'll implement that.\n\nThus, the algorithm:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    items = case['payload']['items']\n    required = set(case['payload']['required_failure_items'])\n    # no mutation\n    current = list(items)\n    attempts = 0\n    n = 2  # number of subsets (granularity)\n    while n <= len(current):\n        # partition current into n contiguous subsets (chunks) as evenly as possible.\n        # For simplicity, we can compute chunk size = ceil(len(current)/n), but typical ddmin uses floor and then left extra.\n        # We'll use the algorithm from \"The Delta Debugging Algorithm\" (Zeller): \n        # split into n subsets, each of size roughly len/n, with the remainder distributed.\n        # We'll iterate over each subset index.\n        size = (len(current) + n - 1) // n  # ceil division\n        # Actually to get exactly n subsets, we need to handle remainder.\n        # Standard: compute base = len // n, rem = len % n. Then first rem subsets have size base+1, rest base.\n        # We'll do that.\n        base = len(current) // n\n        rem = len(current) % n\n        subsets = []\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            part_size = base + (1 if i < rem else 0)\n            subset = current[start:start+part_size]\n            subsets.append(subset)\n            start += part_size\n        # Now try removal of each subset in order (left to right)\n        removed = False\n        for subset in subsets:\n            # construct new list without this subset\n            # we need to remove exactly that contiguous block; we can do by index range\n            # We have start and end for each subset; we need those indices.\n            # Better: while iterating, keep index positions.\n        # We'll compute indices during subset creation.\n        indices = []  # list of (start, end)\n        start = 0\n        for i in range(n):\n            part_size = base + (1 if i < rem else 0)\n            end = start + part_size\n            indices.append((start, end))\n            start = end\n        # Now try removal\n        for (s, e) in indices:\n            new_list = current[:s] + current[e:]\n            attempts += 1\n            # check if required items are all still present\n            if all(item in new_list for item in required):\n                # accept\n                current = new_list\n                removed = True\n                break\n        if removed:\n            # Restart with slightly coarser granularity: fewer subsets (larger chunks). So n = n-1, but at least 2.\n            n = max(2, n - 1)  # or n = n-1? \"slightly coarser\" means fewer subsets, so n-1.\n            # But typical ddmin resets n=2 to start over with the new set.\n            # The description says \"Restart after an accepted removal with slightly coarser granularity.\"\n            # That could mean increase chunk size, i.e., decrease n. Let's go with n-1, but ensure n>=2.\n            # However, if n-1 < 2, set n=2.\n            n = n - 1 if n > 2 else 2\n            # Continue loop (with same n? We'll let while condition reevaluate)\n        else:\n            # No removal succeeded, double granularity (more subsets, finer). So n = n * 2.\n            n = n * 2\n        # Continue loop; the while condition will stop if n > len(current)\n    # After loop, we have reduced current.\n    # Now perform one-minimal check: try removing each single item; check if required still